From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ep9
Subject: [PATCH] Add Latin-1 text and code-unit start predicates for
 UTF-16 searches

With the utf16 feature every search tried every position: the start
predicates (a literal prefix, a first-byte set, the anchored start) are
in UTF-8 bytes, so they were turned off for all inputs, and each failed
position ran the full matcher. An embedder whose strings are Latin-1
(one byte per UTF-16 code unit, as in JavaScript engines) also had to
widen each subject to UTF-16 first.

- Latin1Input / Text::Latin1 / find_from_latin1: a UTF-16 string whose
  code units are all below 256, one byte per unit, matched as UCS-2 or
  UTF-16 (case folding as Utf16CharProperties). Its code units are
  bytes, so the byte searchers work on it.
- CompiledRegex::unit_pred: the start predicate in code units: a
  prefix of units below 256 (memmem), a set of first units (a bitmap of
  the units below 256 and one bit for all others; memchr for 1-3
  units), or anchored at the start. UTF-16, UCS-2 and Latin-1 searches
  use it. A UTF-16 search uses a set only when it holds no unit at or
  above 256, so it never starts inside a surrogate pair. The UTF-16
  prefix search compares at most 16 prefix units at each candidate,
  so its uncounted work stays linear in the text; the counted match
  attempt at the candidate checks the rest.

Searches over &str are unchanged.

Test: tests/latin1.rs checks each search (predicates on) against one
match attempt per position (no predicate) for 29 patterns, 5 flag sets
and 27 texts in all three forms. tests/limits.rs checks that a long
prefix search on UTF-16 text polls.
